+++
title: Sidon
description: Capital city of South Governorate, Lebanon
hook: A mound of broken Murex trunculus shells was found near Sidon's southern
  harbour, cracked open for a pigment so rare it became synonymous with
  royalty. Glass and purple dye were the two industries the Phoenician city
  lived on.
standfirst: In antiquity Sidon was a significant Phoenician city, set on a
  mainland promontory with two harbors. Assyrians, Babylonians, Egyptians,
  Persians, Greeks and finally Romans conquered it in turn; under Persian rule
  it eclipsed Tyre to become the paramount city in Phoenicia. The Arabs took
  it, and then the Ottoman Turks.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: On the Mediterranean coast, in Lebanon's South Governorate, of which it
      is the capital
    from: It is located on the [[ Mediterranean | mediterranean-sea ]] coast in
      the [[ South Governorate | south-governorate-lebanon ]], of which it is
      the capital.
  - name: How many people live there
    value: About 80,000 in the city; more than a quarter-million in the
      metropolitan area
    from: Sidon has a population of about 80,000 within the [[ city limits ]],
      while its [[ metropolitan area ]] has more than a quarter-million
      inhabitants.
  - name: Settled
    value: 3rd millennium BC
    from: "settled: 3rd millennium BC"
  - name: How big
    value: 3.02 sq mi (7.82 km2) for the city, 9.7 sq mi (25 km2) for the metro area
    from: "area: city: 3.02 sq mi (7.82 km2) metro: 9.7 sq mi (25 km2)"
  - name: Mayor
    value: Mustafa al-Hijazi (Independent)
    from: "mayor: Mustafa al-Hijazi (Independent)"
  - name: Heritage status
    value: Tentative World Heritage Site, from 1996-07-01
    from: "heritage-designation: value: Tentative World Heritage Site from:
      1996-07-01"
pull-quotes:
  - text: the Sidonians call Hermon Sirion
    attribution: The Book of Deuteronomy (3, 9)
    section: late-bronze-ii
  - text: data also point to an increase in urban poverty especially in Lebanon's
      largest cities suburbs such as Beirut, Tripoli and Saida, as illustrated
      by poverty-driven symptoms (child labour, over-crowding and deteriorated
      environment conditions).
    attribution: A 2013 United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) report
    section: impact-on-sidon-of-regional-underdevelopment
timeline:
  - when: 3rd millennium BC
    what: Sidon settled, according to the city's records.
    from: "settled: 3rd millennium BC"
  - when: c. 3200 BC
    what: Early Bronze Age deposits at Sidon IV, the tell mound of ancient Sidon.
    from: "!!Sidon IV!! is the [[ tell | tell-archaeology ]] mound of ancient
      Sidon with Early [[ Bronze Age ]] (c. 3200 BC) deposits, now located
      underneath the ruined [[ Saint Louis Castle ]] and what are also thought
      to be the ruins of a [[ Roman | ancient-rome ]] theater.[[ ^9 ]]"
  - when: Around 1350 BC
    what: Sidon is part of the Egyptian Empire, ruled by Zimredda of Sidon.
    from: Around 1350 BC, Sidon was part of the [[ Egyptian Empire |
      new-kingdom-of-egypt ]] and ruled by [[ Zimredda of Sidon ]].
  - when: 480 BC
    what: Sidon's navy fights at the Battle of Salamis on the Persian side.
    from: Sidon's navy played a significant role in the [[ Battle of Salamis ]] in
      480 BC, aligning with the Persian fleet against the Greeks.
  - when: 351 BC
    what: Phoenicia invaded by Artaxerxes III.
    from: At the end of the [[ Persian | achaemenid-empire ]] era, in 351 BC,
      Phoenicia was invaded by [[ Artaxerxes III ]].[[ ^16 ]]
  - when: 333 BC
    what: Alexander the Great occupies the city; the Hellenistic period of Sidon's
      history begins.
    from: ending with its occupation by [[ Alexander the Great ]] in 333 BC, and
      the start of the [[ Hellenistic period ]] of Sidon's history.[[ ^16 ]]
  - when: AD 551
    what: After the great earthquake destroys most of the cities of Phoenice, the
      law school of Berytus takes refuge in Sidon.
    from: when the [[ great earthquake of AD 551 | 551-beirut-earthquake ]]
      destroyed most of the cities of [[ Phoenice | phoenice-roman-province
      ]], the [[ law school of Berytus ]] took refuge in Sidon.
  - when: "636"
    what: The Muslim conquest of the Levant reaches Sidon.
    from: The town continued quietly for the next century, until the [[ Muslim
      conquest of the Levant ]] in 636.[[ ^6 ]][[ ^8 ]]
  - when: 4 December 1110
    what: Sidon captured after a siege by King Baldwin I of Jerusalem and King
      Sigurd I of Norway.
    from: On 4 December 1110, Sidon was captured after the [[ siege of Sidon ]], a
      decade after the [[ First Crusade ]], by King [[ Baldwin I of Jerusalem
      ]] and King [[ Sigurd I of Norway ]].
  - when: AD 1228
    what: The Crusaders build the Sidon Sea Castle.
    from: "caption: Sidon Sea Castle, built by the Crusaders in AD 1228"
  - when: "1249"
    what: The Ayyubids destroy the city.
    from: It remained an important Crusader stronghold until it was destroyed by
      the [[ Ayyubids | ayyubid-dynasty ]] in 1249.
  - when: "1260"
    what: Destroyed again by the Mongols led by Kitbuqa.
    from: In 1260, it was again destroyed by the [[ Mongols ]] led by [[ Kitbuqa
      ]].[[ ^24 ]]
  - when: 26 September 1840
    what: Admiral Charles Napier bombards Sidon; the city capitulates in two days.
    from: bombarded Sidon on 26 September 1840, and landed with a column. Sidon
      capitulated in two days, and the British went on to [[ Acre |
      acre-israel ]].
  - when: AD 1855
    what: The sarcophagus of King Eshmun’azar II is discovered.
    from: In AD 1855, the sarcophagus of King [[ Eshmun’azar | eshmunazar-ii ]] II
      was discovered.
  - when: "1887"
    what: The Ottomans begin extensive excavation of the Royal necropolis of
      Sidon, sending its treasures to Istanbul.
    from: From 1887 the [[ Royal necropolis of Sidon | royal-necropolis-of-ayaa ]]
      was extensively excavated by the Ottomans, and its treasures transferred
      to [[ Istanbul ]] (like the [[ Alexander sarcophagus ]]).
  - when: "1900"
    what: Sidon is a small fishing town of 10,000 inhabitants.
    from: Sidon was a small fishing town of 10,000 inhabitants in 1900.
  - when: 1943-11-22
    what: The date from which the city's records give its country as Lebanon.
    from: "country: value: Lebanon from: 1943-11-22"
  - when: 19 April 1981
    what: At least sixteen people killed when SLA long-range artillery shells the
      city centre on Easter Sunday.
    from: On [[ Easter Sunday | easter-sunday#eastern-christianity ]], 19 April
      1981, at least sixteen people were killed in Sidon after the ([[ South
      Lebanon Army ]]) SLA's long-range artillery indiscriminately shelled the
      city centre.
  - when: 18 August 1997
    what: SLA artillery shells Sidon, killing seven civilians and wounding
      thirty-five.
    from: On 18 August 1997, following a roadside bomb near [[ Jezzine ]] which
      killed two teenage members of a [[ SLA | south-lebanon-army ]] leader's
      family, SLA artillery shelled Sidon killing seven civilians and wounding
      thirty-five.
  - when: 8 June 1999
    what: Two gunmen shoot dead three magistrates and a chief prosecutor in
      Sidon's main courthouse.
    from: On 8 June 1999, two gunmen entered the Palais de Justice, Sidon's main
      courthouse, and shot dead three magistrates and a chief prosecutor.
  - when: 1996-07-01
    what: Sidon's heritage designation as a Tentative World Heritage Site dates
      from here.
    from: "heritage-designation: value: Tentative World Heritage Site from:
      1996-07-01"
  - when: "2013"
    what: The privately funded IBC Enviro treatment plant becomes operational.
    from: He established the privately funded IBC Enviro and the treatment plant
      became operational in 2013.[[ ^39 ]]
  - when: "2022"
    what: Population recorded as 163554.
    from: 'population: - value: "200000" - value: "163554" when: "2022"'
image-plan:
  - file: SidonSeaCastle.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "[[ Sidon Sea Castle ]], built by the [[ Crusaders | crusader-states
      ]] in AD 1228, near the Port of Sidon."
  - file: Protome_from_Eshmun_Sanctuary.jpg
    role: inline
    section: iron-age
    caption: A Persian-style bull [[ protome ]] found in Sidon gives testimony of
      the [[ Achaemenid | achaemenid-empire ]] rule and influence. Marble, 5th
      century BC.
  - file: Archäologisches_Museum_Istanbul_2013-03-18y.jpg
    role: inline
    section: persian-and-hellenistic-periods
    caption: The Tomb of the Mourning Women, from the Hellenistic [[ necropolis ]]
      of Sidon, now in the [[ Istanbul Archaeology Museums ]].
  - file: Roman_era_Phoenecia.png
    role: inline
    section: roman-period
    caption: The [[ Peutinger Table | tabula-peutingeriana ]], showing where Tyre
      and Sidon sat within the Roman Empire.
  - file: Sidon-coast.jpg
    role: full
    section: after-world-war-i
    caption: Sidon with a view of the Mediterranean coast.
  - file: A_Syrian_armored_personnel_carrier_patrols_the_streets_of_Lebanon,_1978.jpg
    role: inline
    section: after-world-war-i
    caption: A Syrian BTR-152 armoured personnel carrier patrols the streets of
      the Lebanese port city of Saida (Sidon), March 1978.
  - file: ZiddonPt1982b.jpg
    role: inline
    section: politics
    caption: Israeli soldiers in the port of Sidon, June 1982. Sidon was occupied
      by the [[ Israeli army | israel-defence-force ]] for almost two and a
      half years after the 1982 invasion.[[ ^34 ]]
  - file: Carpsaida.jpg
    role: inline
    section: main-sights
    caption: An alleyway inside the Old Souks, in the old district of Sidon.
  - file: Alleyways,_Old_city_of_Sidon,_Lebanon.jpg
    role: drop
    section: main-sights
    caption: Alleyways of the Old City of Sidon — dropped as a near-duplicate of
      the Old Souks alleyway above.
  - file: Shrinepeter.jpg
    role: inline
    section: in-the-bible
    caption: Shrine commemorating the last meeting place between [[ St. Paul ]]
      and [[ St. Peter ]] inside the Old City of Sidon.
  - file: Lebanon_districts_Sidon.png
    role: inline
    section: local-government
    caption: The Sidon District, of which the city is the center.
glossary:
  - term: Ṣīdūn
    gloss: The Phoenician name of the city; it probably meant "fishery" or
      "fishing town".
    from: The [[ Phoenician | phoenician-language ]] name //Ṣīdūn// (𐤑𐤃𐤍, ṣdn)
      probably meant "fishery" or "fishing town".[[ ^5 ]]
  - term: Murex trunculus
    gloss: The shellfish broken open for Sidon's purple pigment; discarded shells
      piled up in a mound near the southern harbor.
    from: The magnitude of Sidon's purple dye production was evident through a
      considerable mound of discarded //[[ Murex trunculus ]]// shells
      discovered near the southern harbor.[[ ^6 ]] These shells were broken to
      extract the precious pigment, so rare that it became synonymous with
      royalty.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^12 ]]
  - term: metropolis
    gloss: "'Mother City' — a title Sidon and Tyre each claimed for Phoenicia."
    from: there was competition between the two, each claiming to be the
      metropolis ('Mother City') of [[ Phoenicia ]].
  - term: tell
    gloss: The mound of an ancient city; Sidon IV is the tell of ancient Sidon,
      with deposits from c. 3200 BC.
    from: "!!Sidon IV!! is the [[ tell | tell-archaeology ]] mound of ancient
      Sidon with Early [[ Bronze Age ]] (c. 3200 BC) deposits"
  - term: Diadochi
    gloss: The successors of Alexander, under whom Sidon enjoyed relative autonomy.
    from: Under the [[ Diadochi ]] or successors of Alexander, it enjoyed relative
      autonomy and organised games and competitions in which the greatest
      athletes of the region participated.
  - term: Colonia Aurelia Pia Sidon
    gloss: The name Sidon was given as a Roman colony, honouring its imperial
      sponsor.
    from: As a [[ Roman colony | colonia-roman ]], it was renamed //Colonia
      Aurelia Pia Sidon// to honour [[ its imperial sponsor | elagabalus ]].
  - term: Sidon Eyalet
    gloss: The Ottoman province whose capital Sidon became after coming under
      Ottoman rule in the early 16th century.
    from: After Sidon came under [[ Ottoman Turkish | ottoman-empire ]] rule in
      the early 16th century, it became the capital of the [[ Sidon Eyalet ]]
      (province) and regained a great deal of its earlier commercial
      importance.[[ ^28 ]]
  - term: Makab
    gloss: "What locals called the city's rubbish mountain: a 600,000 cubic metre
      heap the height of a four-story building, near the southern entrance."
    from: Near the southern entrance to the city used to be a 'rubbish mountain'
      called at the time by the locals the //Makab//; namely, a 600,000 cubic
      metre heap that reached the height of a four-story building.
further:
  - slug: tyre-lebanon
    why: Tyre was founded by a colonising party from Sidon, then competed with it
      for centuries over which was the mother city of Phoenicia.
  - slug: byblos
    why: Rib-Hadda's Byblos was the coast's dominant city before the Amarna period
      weakened it — and the place whose old quarter has been rehabilitated
      where Sidon's has not.
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: His occupation of the city in 333 BC opens the Hellenistic period of
      Sidon's history, and the Alexander Sarcophagus came out of its
      necropolis.
anchors:
  hook: "During the Phoenician era, Sidon thrived on two pivotal industries:
    glass manufacturing and [[ purple dye | tyrian-purple ]] production. The
    city's glass production operated on an extensive scale, while the
    manufacturing of purple dye held nearly equal importance.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^12
    ]] The magnitude of Sidon's purple dye production was evident through a
    considerable mound of discarded //[[ Murex trunculus ]]// shells
    discovered near the southern harbor.[[ ^6 ]] These shells were broken to
    extract the precious pigment, so rare that it became synonymous with
    royalty.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^12 ]]"
  standfirst: "In antiquity, Sidon was a significant [[ Phoenician | phoenicia
    ]] city. It was nestled on a mainland promontory and had two harbors.[[ ^6
    ]] Throughout ancient history, Sidon had many conquerors: [[ Assyrians |
    neo-assyrian-empire ]], [[ Babylonians | neo-babylonian-empire ]], [[
    Egyptians | ancient-egypt ]], [[ Persians | achaemenid-empire ]], [[
    Greeks | ancient-greece ]], and finally [[ Romans | ancient-rome ]]. Under
    Persian rule, it eclipsed [[ Tyre | tyre-lebanon ]] to become the
    paramount city in Phoenicia.[[ ^6 ]] In the New Testament era, [[ Herod
    the Great ]] visited Sidon. Both [[ Jesus ]] and [[ Saint Paul |
    paul-of-tarsus ]] are said to have visited it, too (see //[[ Biblical
    Sidon | #biblical-sidon ]]// below). The city was eventually conquered by
    the [[ Arabs | muslim-conquest-of-the-levant ]] and then by the [[ Ottoman
    Turks | ottoman-empire ]].[[ ^7 ]][[ ^8 ]]"
